[h1]Cities and Borders[/h1]
The stock tactical map only shows coastlines. This mod adds the national borders, capitals, cities and naval bases of the mission's date: the Soviet Union, East and West Germany, Yugoslavia, Leningrad and Bonn, Holy Loch and Cam Ranh Bay.

[b]It only draws on the map. Nothing in the game or in a mission changes, and the lines are a reference map, not the rules of your mission.[/b] Please read the part about that further down before you trust a line in a tense mission.

[h2]What you get[/h2]
[list]
[*]Land borders as they were on the mission's date. The Soviet Union is one country (the Baltic states split off in September 1991, the other republics in December 1991). The inner-German border and West Berlin are there until October 1990, Yugoslavia until 1991/92, Czechoslovakia until 1993, North and South Yemen until May 1990, the Vietnam DMZ until 1976. Eritrea, South Sudan, East Timor, Hong Kong and Macau change in their years. Disputed borders and lines of control (Korea, Kashmir, the Golan, Cyprus) are dashed.
[*]Capitals of the time, so East Berlin and Bonn before October 1990, and Kiev or Riga only become national capitals when the Union ends. City names follow the date too: Leningrad, Gorky, Karl-Marx-Stadt, Bombay, Peking before 1979.
[*]Naval bases with an anchor in their side's colour (blue for NATO and its allies, red for the Warsaw Pact), plus air bases. A base only shows in the years it was used: Holy Loch until 1992, Cam Ranh Bay as a Soviet base 1979 to 2002, Kings Bay from 1979.
[*]An optional coastal distance line, off by default. It's a dashed line 3, 6 or 12 nm off the coast as the game's map draws it, islands included.
[*]Lines keep their width at every zoom, names don't overlap, and there's a lighter colour set for the night map. Unit symbols, waypoints and the map's own labels stay on top.
[/list]

[h2]Read this first: what the lines are and aren't[/h2]
The mod doesn't touch rules of engagement, triggers, zones or scoring, and it doesn't change what your units see or do.

Missions set their own areas and rules, and those often don't match real geography. [i]Northern Vigil[/i] goes weapons free when two Soviet vessels enter its own 10 nm circle around a point, which has nothing to do with the coast or a border. [i]Don't Mess With Texas[/i] fails you for attacking an airbase. [i]Operation Trident[/i] has an exclusion zone off Karachi. When a briefing says "don't escalate" or "hold fire", go by the briefing and the mission's own map areas (Map Layers, On Map Areas in the game), not by anything this mod draws. A ship crossing one of these lines is not a violation in the game unless the mission says so, and the same goes the other way round.

The borders are today's lines with date rules on top, simplified, and disputed ones are drawn the way Natural Earth draws them. Land borders only: no sea borders, no airspace, no zones. The Berlin Wall and the Vietnam DMZ I drew by hand, so they're approximate.

The coastal distance line is not a territorial limit. It's one distance from the game's coastline, the same for every country. In 1985 Norway claimed 4 nm, the US and the UK 3, Greece and Turkey 6 in the Aegean, the Soviet Union 12, and Libya the whole Gulf of Sidra. Real limits are also measured from baselines across bays and fjords, not from the shore. Treat it as a ruler.

Cities, capitals and bases come from the game's own lists plus public data. A base on the map doesn't mean the mission models it, and the labels are public Cold War locations, not intelligence about the mission's forces. Units and contacts are exactly what the game shows.

[h2]Where the data comes from[/h2]
[list]
[*]Land borders: [url=https://www.naturalearthdata.com/]Natural Earth[/url] 1:10m boundary lines, public domain (release 5.1.2). Which border existed when is my own work.
[*]Inner-German border, North/South Yemen and West Berlin: built from Natural Earth's state lines (the old Länder and today's governorates). Berlin Wall and Vietnam DMZ: hand-drawn.
[*]Cities, ports and airfields: the game's campaign lists, read from your install when a mission starts. None of the game's data is copied or shipped.
[*]More cities and capitals: Natural Earth populated places, public domain. These carry today's populations, so the ranking of cities in 1985 is a bit off.
[*]Naval bases with side and years: my own list, positions approximate.
[*]Coastal distance line: worked out in the game from its own world grid. No game terrain is shipped.
[/list]
Made with Natural Earth. Free vector and raster map data at naturalearthdata.com.

[h2]How to use[/h2]
Open the map in a mission. The layers are in the map menu (≡), Map Layers, [b]Borders[/b] and [b]Cities and bases[/b], or use Shift+F7 for borders and F7 for cities. The coastal distance line stays off until you set [i]Coastal distance line (nm)[/i] to 3, 6 or 12 in the config.

[h2]What may come[/h2]
If people like it I'd like to go further, for example drawing each country's borders and sea limits the way that country defined them back then (in 1985 the Soviet Union claimed 12 nm of territorial sea, Norway 4, the US 3) instead of one distance for everybody. It would still be a reference and not mission rules. Ideas and corrections are welcome in the comments.

[h2]Requirements[/h2]
[b]Anchor Chain[/b] is required: [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3380210757]subscribe[/url] and follow its [url=https://seapower-modders.github.io/AnchorChain/install-anchor-chain.html]installation guide[/url] (one manual step: copy its preloader into the game folder).

[h2]Settings[/h2]
[i]<Sea Power>\BepInEx\config\dev.sewerlabs.seapowerborders.cfg[/i]. Borders: toggle key, era (mission date or today), disputed lines, line width, opacity, coastal distance line. Cities: toggle key, density, naval bases, air bases, civil airports, label size.

[h2]Compatibility[/h2]
Sea Power 0.8.7. Tested with Topographic Map, Better TacMap, Rats Radio and Rats Pop-Out Windows loaded.

The 3D globe map of the 11 November update: the package has a second DLL for it (the public beta of 26 October 2026). I ran it in the game on that beta, but Anchor Chain 1.1.0 doesn't start there yet, so players can only use the mod on the beta once Anchor Chain is updated. Each DLL stays off on the game version it isn't for and the log says which one is running. Leave both in the folder.

No game files are changed. If a game update breaks one of the mod's hooks, it says so in the log and stays out of the way.

[h2]Problems?[/h2]
Please post [i]<Sea Power>\BepInEx\LogOutput.log[/i] with your report, and tell me the mission and its start date.

Source code (MIT): [url=https://github.com/struckl/SeaPowerBorders]github.com/struckl/SeaPowerBorders[/url]
